WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.532
Coding Exercise 1 focuses on a simple SELECT and sort.

00:00:03.532 --> 00:00:08.267
The required output contains medical record number, patient name, and age.

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:11.677
The rows should be ordered from the oldest patient to the youngest.

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:16.472
Before typing, separate the task into two parts: which columns should appear,

00:00:16.472 --> 00:00:18.432
and what order should the rows use?

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:21.882
Write your SQL directly in the browser and use Run and Check.

00:00:21.882 --> 00:00:26.602
If the query runs but does not pass, inspect the result columns and row order.

00:00:26.602 --> 00:00:31.727
Make sure you are not returning unnecessary columns.

00:00:31.727 --> 00:00:33.762
Do not worry about filters yet.

00:00:33.762 --> 00:00:40.572
This exercise is intentionally focused on selecting the correct columns and using a descending age sort.

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:43.607
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:43.607 --> 00:00:48.062
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:48.062 --> 00:00:50.977
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:55.597
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:55.597 --> 00:01:00.797
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:00.797 --> 00:01:07.167
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.450
not only whether the statement executes.
